<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1809">1809</a>; music <a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Der gute Kamerad, Ich hatt' einen Kameraden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Der gute Kamerad&quot; (&quot;The Good Comrade&quot;), better known by its opening line as &quot;Ich hatt' einen Kameraden&quot; (&quot;I had a comrade&quot;), is a German soldiers' lament. The German romantic poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-uhland">Ludwig Uhland</a> wrote the words in 1809; their immediate inspiration was the sending of troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> against the Tyrolean Rebellion.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Der gute Kamerad&quot; (&quot;The Good Comrade&quot;), better known by its opening line as &quot;<b>Ich hatt' einen Kameraden</b>&quot; (&quot;I had a comrade&quot;), is a German soldiers' lament.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</a></li></ul></section>
